WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.600
Some cloud permissions are risky because they allow a principal to create, modify,

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:07.140
or impersonate other identities.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.080
The danger is not always obvious from the role name.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:14.060
A principal may not be labeled administrator, but if it can attach policies,

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:20.200
create access keys, update trust relationships, pass roles, impersonate service accounts,

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.580
or modify deployment automation,

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.400
it may be able to reach more privilege than the visible assignment suggests.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.340
Defensive IAM review should look for permission paths.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.200
Can this identity grant itself more access?

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.900
Can it change a role that another workload trusts?

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.960
Can it edit a function, startup script, container image,

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.740
or pipeline that runs with stronger permissions?

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.440
Can it create credentials for another identity?

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:47.140
Can it disable logging or security controls?

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.760
These questions are design and review questions, not instructions for abuse.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:55.260
They help teams find paths that need guardrails.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.720
Deployment systems deserve special care.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:03.140
A CI/CD pipeline may be able to update infrastructure, deploy functions,

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.560
modify containers, or change cloud configuration.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.940
If that pipeline has broad permissions,

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.920
then anyone who can change the pipeline definition, build artifact,

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.900
or deployment script may indirectly influence privileged cloud actions.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:22.240
Secure design separates duties, scopes deployment roles, reviews pipeline access,

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.700
and monitors sensitive changes.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.400
The practical goal is to reduce surprise.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:32.500
IAM review should not stop at role names like reader, developer, operator, or deployer.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.760
It should examine sensitive actions, trust relationships, impersonation, role passing,

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:41.660
policy changes, credential creation, and logging changes.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:47.320
When risky paths are necessary, they should be documented, approved, monitored,

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:51.940
and constrained so that legitimate administration does not become uncontrolled privilege

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:52.960
growth.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:56.700
Risky permission paths are often found at the edges between teams.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.480
A developer may not be a cloud administrator,

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.300
but may edit a pipeline that uses an administrator-like deployment role.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:05.920
A support identity may not own policies,

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:09.420
but may be able to create keys for a powerful service account.

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:12.120
A function editor may not own data directly,

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.300
but may change code that runs with data access.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.320
These indirect paths deserve the same seriousness as direct grants.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:24.380
Defensive controls can include permission boundaries, approval workflows,

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.640
restricted role passing, separate deployment roles, policy review, protected branches,

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:33.620
mandatory code review, and alerts on sensitive IAM changes.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.800
The exact control depends on the platform and workflow.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:40.060
The important habit is to ask how privilege can move,

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:42.440
not only where privilege appears to sit today.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:46.580
Logging and alerting help make risky paths manageable.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.680
A permission may be necessary for a deployment platform,

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.140
but sensitive use should still be visible.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:58.600
Alerts on policy attachment, trust changes, key creation, role passing, impersonation,

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:03.700
and security logging changes give defenders a chance to spot unexpected behavior before it

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:05.840
becomes a larger incident.
